          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-df3cbc9370004c4a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"Antwan’s “I love you” primarily avows his loving emotional state. In conjunction with “I want to be with you,” it also helps enact a bid to resume or establish a relationship, giving moderate performative support, although it does not itself make a definite promise. Nothing indicates that the words escape as a reflex-like affective discharge; emotional urgency and vulnerability alone do not establish E.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The relational appeal creates some uncertainty over P: the words participate in courtship and reconciliation, but the explicit surrounding language expresses desire and requests contact rather than undertaking a firm commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"Antwan’s previously recorded voicemail is played by Nikki and reproduced as direct quoted speech by the narrator.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal occurs within one of 22 voicemails seeking renewed contact. Antwan acknowledges that the situation is complicated, says he wants to be with Nikki, and asks her to call back, so the declaration functions both as an avowal and as part of a persuasive relational appeal. The narration treats love and vulnerability as real while also emphasizing his existing girlfriend, children, and responsibilities, which leads Nikki to reject the relationship. That fraught and potentially pressuring context does not alter the core T/P classification or independently support E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I can’t stop thinking about you, Nikki. I love you.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration presents Antwan’s love as true and is therefore strongly truth-conditional.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I know it’s complicated, but I want to be with you.”\",\"supports\":\"The declaration is embedded in a bid for a relationship, providing moderate performative force while stopping short of an explicit promise.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I’m not asking you to make a decision now, but please, just… call me back.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate practical goal is renewed contact; this confirms persuasive courtship context but weakens the case for a definite relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Nikki finds 22 voicemails from Antwan, and the narration later describes urgency and vulnerability in the sequence and says, “There was love in his words. Real love.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrative frames the declarations as sincere-seeming avowals within an emotionally urgent campaign, while supplying no evidence of reflexive or uncontrolled verbal production.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is an affirmative present-tense explicit “I love you” in Ania Cofield’s Nikki’s Touch.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified occurrence and its canonical linguistic form.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the avowal, while P captures its secondary role in a relational bid. Emotional urgency remains contextual delivery evidence and does not require E; no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
